{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_KzKIZP2cAs1dQQQnsDulRT8I","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# session-protocol Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→Define the session lifecycle protocol for SE 3.0 agents. This spec governs progressive startup (se3 status → progress.md → scope determination), execution boundaries, shutdown procedures, and tool-enforced progress tracking across sessions.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Session Startup Protocol\n     7→The system SHALL define a progressive session startup protocol. The agent MUST locate current state with minimal context, then load more on demand.\n     8→\n     9→Startup steps:\n    10→1. Verify `openspec` CLI is available; if not, ask the human to install it. If available but `openspec/` directory does not exist, run `openspec init`.\n    11→2. Run `se3 status` for computed project state (git, collab, human-calls)\n    12→3. Read `head -50 progress.md` + `git log --oneline -5`\n    13→4. Scan `human-calls/` for responded but unprocessed requests\n    14→5. Check `openspec/changes/` for active changes and `openspec/specs/` for current capabilities\n    15→6. Determine session scope based on progress \"next steps\" + active changes\n    16→7. Load additional files only when the task requires them\n    17→\n    18→If step 3 finds no progress.md and no git history → **first-time bootstrap**:\n    19→- Ask the human (sync human call): \"What should this project do?\"\n    20→- Create an openspec change from the response (proposal captures the intent)\n    21→- Create `progress.md`\n    22→\n    23→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not installed\n    24→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` command is not found\n    25→- **THEN** agent asks the human to install it via sync human call and does not proceed with spec-related work until resolved\n    26→\n    27→#### Scenario: OpenSpec not initialized\n    28→- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` is available but `openspec/` directory does not exist\n    29→- **THEN** agent runs `openspec init` before proceeding\n    30→\n    31→#### Scenario: Mature project startup\n    32→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with existing progress.md and git history\n    33→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 status`, reads progress.md and git log, then determines scope without reading all spec files upfront\n    34→\n    35→#### Scenario: First-time bootstrap\n    36→- **WHEN** agent starts a session with no progress.md and no git history\n    37→- **THEN** agent asks the human what to build via human call, creates an openspec change from the response\n    38→\n    39→#### Scenario: On-demand deep loading\n    40→- **WHEN** agent needs details about a specific capability during execution\n    41→- **THEN** agent reads the relevant spec file at that point, not during startup\n    42→\n    43→### Requirement: Session Execution Boundary\n    44→Each session MUST focus on a limited scope of work and MUST NOT attempt to complete too many tasks in a single session.\n    45→\n    46→#### Scenario: Session scope limitation\n    47→- **WHEN** the agent has determined the work scope through the startup protocol\n    48→- **THEN** the agent only executes tasks within that scope and does not actively expand the scope\n    49→\n    50→### Requirement: Session Shutdown Protocol\n    51→Session ending MUST leave code in a mergeable state. Progress tracking is handled automatically by tools.\n    52→\n    53→Shutdown steps:\n    54→1. Ensure all modified code runs correctly\n    55→2. Run `se3 handoff` — automatically commits and generates session summary in progress.md\n    56→3. Update openspec change status if applicable\n    57→\n    58→### Requirement: Session Commit Cadence\n    59→\n    60→The system SHALL define when commits occur during a session.\n    61→\n    62→Commits SHOULD occur mid-session when a distinct, working unit of change is complete — not accumulated into a single commit with unrelated changes.\n    63→\n    64→**When to commit mid-session:**\n    65→- After completing a coherent unit of work that passes tests\n    66→- Before starting a substantially different task that would muddy the commit message\n    67→- Before context clearing (/new) if there are completed changes to preserve\n    68→\n    69→**Commit sequence:**\n    70→1. Run tests — do NOT commit with failing tests\n    71→2. Stage files\n    72→3. Write message with context for next session\n    73→4. Commit (via `se3 commit` — automatically appends progress entry)\n    74→\n    75→**Commit Rules:**\n    76→- Commit when a meaningful unit of work is complete — not tied to /new or any mechanical trigger\n    77→- Do not batch unrelated changes into one commit\n    78→- Commit messages MUST include summary of changes and context for the next session\n    79→\n    80→#### Scenario: Mid-session commit\n    81→- **WHEN** a distinct unit of work is complete and tested\n    82→- **THEN** agent commits before starting the next unit\n    83→\n    84→### Requirement: Session Context Clearing\n    85→\n    86→The system SHALL define when context is cleared during a session.\n    87→\n    88→Context SHOULD be cleared when it approaches saturation or when switching to a substantially different task.\n    89→\n    90→Context SHOULD NOT be cleared mechanically after every task group — agents SHOULD continue if there is context budget and the next task benefits from current context.\n    91→\n    92→#### Scenario: Normal shutdown\n    93→- **WHEN** agent completes the current scope\n    94→- **THEN** agent runs `se3 handoff`, code is mergeable, progress is updated automatically\n    95→\n    96→#### Scenario: Context approaching saturation\n    97→- **WHEN** context window is nearly full\n    98→- **THEN** agent prioritizes shutdown protocol (at minimum: `se3 commit` current work)\n    99→\n   100→### Requirement: Progress Tracking File\n   101→The system SHALL use progress.md as the cumulative cross-session progress record. Progress entries are appended automatically by `se3 commit` and `se3 handoff` — agents do NOT manually maintain progress.md.\n   102→\n   103→progress.md contains:\n   104→- Auto-appended commit entries in \"Current Session\" section (by `se3 commit`)\n   105→- Formal session records generated by `se3 handoff`\n   106→- Collab session reports generated by orchestrator do_complete()\n   107→\n   108→#### Scenario: Auto-append commit entry\n   109→- **WHEN** `se3 commit` succeeds\n   110→- **THEN** a one-line commit record is automatically appended to progress.md Current Session section\n   111→\n   112→#### Scenario: Finalize session\n   113→- **WHEN** `se3 handoff` is executed\n   114→- **THEN** the Current Session section is replaced with a formal dated session record including commits, files changed, and next steps\n   115→\n   116→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e569dacb-b70b-4c90-930f-0ca840cf5264","uuid":"61f84c69-5e7f-4da4-a249-e429ba5216c3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/.worktrees/verify-openspec-commands/openspec/specs/session-protocol/spec.md","content":"# session-protocol Specification\n\n## Purpose\nDefine the session lifecycle protocol for SE 3.0 agents. This spec governs progressive startup (se3 status → progress.md → scope determination), execution boundaries, shutdown procedures, and tool-enforced progress tracking across sessions.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Session Startup Protocol\nThe system SHALL define a progressive session startup protocol. The agent MUST locate current state with minimal context, then load more on demand.\n\nStartup steps:\n1. Verify `openspec` CLI is available; if not, ask the human to install it. If available but `openspec/` directory does not exist, run `openspec init`.\n2. Run `se3 status` for computed project state (git, collab, human-calls)\n3. Read `head -50 progress.md` + `git log --oneline -5`\n4. Scan `human-calls/` for responded but unprocessed requests\n5. Check `openspec/changes/` for active changes and `openspec/specs/` for current capabilities\n6. Determine session scope based on progress \"next steps\" + active changes\n7. Load additional files only when the task requires them\n\nIf step 3 finds no progress.md and no git history → **first-time bootstrap**:\n- Ask the human (sync human call): \"What should this project do?\"\n- Create an openspec change from the response (proposal captures the intent)\n- Create `progress.md`\n\n#### Scenario: OpenSpec not installed\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` command is not found\n- **THEN** agent asks the human to install it via sync human call and does not proceed with spec-related work until resolved\n\n#### Scenario: OpenSpec not initialized\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session and `openspec` is available but `openspec/` directory does not exist\n- **THEN** agent runs `openspec init` before proceeding\n\n#### Scenario: Mature project startup\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session with existing progress.md and git history\n- **THEN** agent runs `se3 status`, reads progress.md and git log, then determines scope without reading all spec files upfront\n\n#### Scenario: First-time bootstrap\n- **WHEN** agent starts a session with no progress.md and no git history\n- **THEN** agent asks the human what to build via human call, creates an openspec change from the response\n\n#### Scenario: On-demand deep loading\n- **WHEN** agent needs details about a specific capability during execution\n- **THEN** agent reads the relevant spec file at that point, not during startup\n\n### Requirement: Session Execution Boundary\nEach session MUST focus on a limited scope of work and MUST NOT attempt to complete too many tasks in a single session.\n\n#### Scenario: Session scope limitation\n- **WHEN** the agent has determined the work scope through the startup protocol\n- **THEN** the agent only executes tasks within that scope and does not actively expand the scope\n\n### Requirement: Session Shutdown Protocol\nSession ending MUST leave code in a mergeable state. Progress tracking is handled automatically by tools.\n\nShutdown steps:\n1. Ensure all modified code runs correctly\n2. Run `se3 handoff` — automatically commits and generates session summary in progress.md\n3. Update openspec change status if applicable\n\n### Requirement: Session Commit Cadence\n\nThe system SHALL define when commits occur during a session.\n\nCommits SHOULD occur mid-session when a distinct, working unit of change is complete — not accumulated into a single commit with unrelated changes.\n\n**When to commit mid-session:**\n- After completing a coherent unit of work that passes tests\n- Before starting a substantially different task that would muddy the commit message\n- Before context clearing (/new) if there are completed changes to preserve\n\n**Commit sequence:**\n1. Run tests — do NOT commit with failing tests\n2. Stage files\n3. Write message with context for next session\n4. Commit (via `se3 commit` — automatically appends progress entry)\n\n**Commit Rules:**\n- Commit when a meaningful unit of work is complete — not tied to /new or any mechanical trigger\n- Do not batch unrelated changes into one commit\n- Commit messages MUST include summary of changes and context for the next session\n\n#### Scenario: Mid-session commit\n- **WHEN** a distinct unit of work is complete and tested\n- **THEN** agent commits before starting the next unit\n\n### Requirement: Session Context Clearing\n\nThe system SHALL define when context is cleared during a session.\n\nContext SHOULD be cleared when it approaches saturation or when switching to a substantially different task.\n\nContext SHOULD NOT be cleared mechanically after every task group — agents SHOULD continue if there is context budget and the next task benefits from current context.\n\n#### Scenario: Normal shutdown\n- **WHEN** agent completes the current scope\n- **THEN** agent runs `se3 handoff`, code is mergeable, progress is updated automatically\n\n#### Scenario: Context approaching saturation\n- **WHEN** context window is nearly full\n- **THEN** agent prioritizes shutdown protocol (at minimum: `se3 commit` current work)\n\n### Requirement: Progress Tracking File\nThe system SHALL use progress.md as the cumulative cross-session progress record. Progress entries are appended automatically by `se3 commit` and `se3 handoff` — agents do NOT manually maintain progress.md.\n\nprogress.md contains:\n- Auto-appended commit entries in \"Current Session\" section (by `se3 commit`)\n- Formal session records generated by `se3 handoff`\n- Collab session reports generated by orchestrator do_complete()\n\n#### Scenario: Auto-append commit entry\n- **WHEN** `se3 commit` succeeds\n- **THEN** a one-line commit record is automatically appended to progress.md Current Session section\n\n#### Scenario: Finalize session\n- **WHEN** `se3 handoff` is executed\n- **THEN** the Current Session section is replaced with a formal dated session record including commits, files changed, and next steps\n\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
